</example><exercise data-wait-for-event="RFM1">
  <problem>
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    <para>For numbers 1-2, use the equations below:</para>
    <blockquote>
      <para>These are the equations from the activity. Each equation is in the form \(Ax+By=C\)</para>
    </blockquote>
    <para>\( 4x - 3y = -3 \)
      \( 12x - 4y = 8 \)
      \( 8x + 2y = 16 \)
      \( 3x - y = -1\)
      \( 4x - 3y = 6 \)
    </para>
    <para>Observe the graph of the equation \(−4x+3y=3\) where it is graphed, on the same coordinate plane, with another line passing through \((0,0)\) and \((A,B)\). In this example \(A=-4\) and \(B=3\), so the other line would pass through \((0,0)\) and \((−4,3)\). What do you notice about the lines?
      </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Answers may vary, but here is a sample.The two lines are perpendicular.
    </para>
    <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d6be84743f72a71b97f9a7e1e00e6c0529fc3a3b"/></media></figure></para>
  </solution></exercise>

